  "body": "## Motivation\n\nRelated to #36207.\n\nSGLang reserves physical KV-cache slot 0 as a finite padding source for CUDA\ngraph and DP-attention padding. Generic KV stores protect that invariant after\n#32477, but MLA-specific writers bypass `store_cache()` and still accept\n`loc == 0` as a write destination.\n\nUndefined graph/DP-padding rows can therefore overwrite slot 0 with NaNs. A\nlater padded attention row may read the poisoned slot and propagate NaNs into\nattention output and model logits. During the GLM-5.2 investigation, affected\nrequests produced short nonsensical output followed by one repeated token.\n\n## Root-cause boundary\n\nThis PR fixes the SGLang-owned MLA writers:\n\n| Writer | Covered here |\n| --- | ---: |\n| BF16 Triton MLA writer | Yes |\n| FP8 quantized MLA writer | Yes |\n| MLA scale writer | Yes |\n| CUDA TMA/JIT MLA writer | Yes |\n| AITER fused GLM rope/cat/cache writer | No |\n\nThe fused ROCm path through `aiter.fused_qk_rope_cat_and_cache_mla` writes KV\ninternally and then invokes attention with `save_kv_cache=False`. It bypasses\nthe writers changed here and requires a separate API-safe integration fix.\nThis PR should therefore not be interpreted as resolving the complete GLM ROCm\npath by itself.\n\n## Modifications\n\n- Add `reserved_skip_index=0` to the Triton MLA data, FP8-quantized, and\n  scale-buffer writers.\n- Apply the same runtime guard to the CUDA TMA bulk-store writer.\n- Preserve static launch shapes and DCP ownership behavior; no Python-side\n  compaction is introduced.\n- Support `reserved_skip_index=-1` to opt out, matching `store_cache()`\n  semantics.\n- Add focused CUDA and AMD regression tests.\n\n## Deterministic reproduction\n\nInitialize slot 0 to a finite sentinel and invoke an MLA writer with\n`loc=[0, 2]`. The source row mapped to slot 0 may be NaN because it represents\npadding, while the row mapped to slot 2 is valid.\n\nWithout this patch:\n\n- slot 0 is overwritten by the padding row;\n- the valid positive slot is written normally.\n\nWith this patch:\n\n- slot 0 remains byte-for-byte unchanged;\n- the valid positive slot is still written;\n- passing `reserved_skip_index=-1` restores the legacy behavior explicitly.\n\nThe tests exercise this contract directly instead of depending on\nuninitialized allocator contents.\n\n## Accuracy tests\n\n- Added kernel tests for BF16 MLA writes with int32 and int64 locations.\n- Added FP8-quantized and scale-buffer reserved-slot tests.\n- Added CUDA TMA/JIT writer coverage.\n- Added the explicit opt-out path.\n- Python syntax compilation and `git diff --check` pass locally.\n- Local GPU tests were not run for this PR; the focused tests are registered\n  for CUDA and AMD CI.\n\nThe broader real-weight GLM investigation separately demonstrated the same\ninvariant violation in the fused AITER writer: `slot_mapping=[0]` with a NaN\npadding row changed a finite slot 0 into 576 NaNs. That result motivates the\nsystem-level issue but is outside this PR's implementation scope.\n\n## Speed tests and profiling\n\nNot run. The kernels add one scalar comparison to the existing store predicate\nwithout changing launch grids or tensor shapes.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Format code according to the project style.\n- [x] Add focused CUDA and AMD tests.\n- [x] Document the API behavior in the writer docstrings.\n- [x] Preserve existing behavior through an explicit opt-out.\n- [x] Clarify the fused AITER scope boundary.\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:hourglass_flowing_sand: [Run #32921116235](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/32921116235)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #32921116036](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/32921116036)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 7.2): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd-rocm720:start -->:hourglass_flowing_sand: [Run #32921116122](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/32921116122)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd-rocm720:end -->\n<!-- pr-states:end -->",
